-- title:
--   Proposition 4.9 — exhaustion of a hyperfinite graph by Borel pieces with finite classes (generalized to a finite-section $D\subseteq G$)
-- statement:
--   Let $(X,G)$ be a hyperfinite graph on a standard Borel space, and let $D\subseteq G$ be a Borel relation in which every vertex has finitely many $D$-neighbours. Then there is an increasing sequence of Borel sets
--   $$\Omega_1\subseteq\Omega_2\subseteq\Omega_3\subseteq\cdots\subseteq X,\qquad X=\bigcup_n\Omega_n,$$
--   such that for every $n$ the equivalence classes of the relation on $\Omega_n$ generated by $D\cap(\Omega_n\times\Omega_n)$ are finite.
--
--   With $D=G$, for a hyperfinite graph of finite degree, this is the printed proposition. It is what lets Proposition 4.10 reduce a hyperfinite game to a game with finite (hence smooth) components on most of the space.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: a hyperfinite graph G of finite degree; the relation on Ωn generated by G ∩ (Ωn × Ωn) → a hyperfinite graph G of countable degree and a Borel D ⊆ G with finite sections; the relation generated by D ∩ (Ωn × Ωn); reason: D = G recovers the printed statement; the generalized form is what the corrected Lemma 4.8 feeds into Proposition 4.10
--   - generalization: an increasing sequence of subsets Ωn → an increasing sequence of Borel sets Ωn; reason: a stronger conclusion: the proof's Ωn are Borel, and Proposition 4.10 needs µ(Ωn)
--   - generalization: the vertex set of a graphical game is a Polish space → the vertex set X is a standard Borel space; reason: every Polish space is standard Borel and no notion used depends on the topology of X; the associated game of §4.2 lives on the type space, which is standard Borel without a named Polish topology
--   - encoding: hyperfinite: generated by a Borel ℤ-action, or alternatively an increasing union of Borel finite equivalence relations (§2.3) → the increasing-union form; reason: the paper states the two forms as equivalent for CBERs (Dougherty–Jackson–Kechris 1994) and its proofs use the increasing-union form
-- source:
--   Hellman–Levy, Equilibria Existence in Bayesian Games: Climbing the Countable Borel Equivalence Relation Hierarchy, author's manuscript (2020-07-14), Proposition 4.9, p.24

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_HellmanLevy_Graphical_IsHyperfiniteGraph

namespace HellmanLevy.Graphical

/-- **Proposition 4.9 (generalized to a finite-section subrelation `D ⊆ G`).** Let `(X, G)` be a
hyperfinite graph and `D ⊆ G` a Borel relation in which every vertex has finitely many
`D`-neighbours. Then there is an increasing sequence of Borel sets `Ω₁ ⊆ Ω₂ ⊆ ⋯` with
`X = ⋃ₙ Ωₙ` such that for each `n` the classes of the equivalence relation on `Ωₙ` generated by
`D ∩ (Ωₙ × Ωₙ)` are finite.
Hellman–Levy, *Equilibria Existence in Bayesian Games: Climbing the Countable Borel Equivalence
Relation Hierarchy*, author's manuscript (2020-07-14), Proposition 4.9, p.24. With `D = G` (a hyperfinite graph of finite degree) this is the
printed statement.

**Formalization Note** The generalization is the form in which Lemma 4.8 (corrected) feeds
Proposition 4.10: there the payoffs depend on a finite-section `D ⊆ G` while `G` itself may have
infinite degree. The sets `Ωₙ` are Borel (the proof's `Ωₙ` are), which Proposition 4.10 needs
for `µ(Ωₙ)`. -/
theorem proposition_4_9 {X : Type*} [MeasurableSpace X] [StandardBorelSpace X]
    (G D : X → X → Prop) (hG : IsHyperfiniteGraph G) (hD : CBER.IsBorelRel D)
    (hDG : ∀ x y, D x y → G x y) (hDfin : ∀ x, {y | D x y}.Finite) :
    ∃ Ωn : ℕ → Set X, (∀ n, MeasurableSet (Ωn n)) ∧ Monotone Ωn ∧ (⋃ n, Ωn n) = Set.univ ∧
      ∀ n, ∀ x ∈ Ωn n,
        {y | Relation.EqvGen (fun a b => a ∈ Ωn n ∧ b ∈ Ωn n ∧ D a b) x y}.Finite := by sorry

end HellmanLevy.Graphical
